    - Froze `tinyrustlm.vnext.durable-publication.v1` as one incompatible production boundary beneath the four stores. It accepts no caller filesystem, platform, randomness, helper, process output, directory-flush result, readback result, compatibility reader, or fallback.
    - Required a preexisting absolute privately owned root. The boundary never claims to have durably created its own root. Missing child directories are created one component at a time and each new entry's parent plus new directory are flushed through the verified Rust helper.
    - Target publication now has one exact primitive: exclusive same-directory temporary, complete file fsync, hard-link create-if-absent, byte-identical existing-target acceptance, helper-backed target-directory flush, and exact bounded readback.
    - A canonical content-addressed durability receipt binds target path/hash/size, store and durability profiles, implementation source closure, file fsync, publication primitive, target readback, and the complete client/helper/package/request/observation identity chain.
    - The durability receipt is the explicit terminal persistence trust anchor. It excludes its own publication observation to avoid a self-hash cycle or infinite receipt chain. Creation still requires receipt file fsync, create-only publication, helper-backed receipt-directory flush, exact receipt readback, and a second exact target readback.
    - Restart activation requires a matching current receipt, fresh helper-backed target and receipt directory flushes, and exact readbacks. A target without a receipt, a receipt without a target, profile drift, noncanonical bytes, unknown fields, and target bit drift remain inactive.
    - Repeated byte-identical publication derives the same receipt in the same verified environment. Multiple equivalent receipts from independently verified helper packages select the lexicographically smallest receipt SHA without mutable pointers or best-of-N truth.
    - ADR-012 and the maintained durability documentation record the bootstrap decision and explicit nonclaims.
