      verification: The complete release-surface suite passed 21/21 before the phase-parent correction; the final deployment admission pair then passed 2/2 and the phase-currentness suite passed 10/10, including a direct `current-pass` assertion over the web-build/deployment graph. The contract/inventory/threat set passed 12/12. Final complete `npm run test:unit` over the corrected bytes passed 860/860 with zero failures, skips, cancellations, or todos in 378.264 seconds. JavaScript syntax, strict JSON parsing, and `git diff --check` passed.
      documentation_and_deployment_truth: The release-surface architecture, contract guidance, and contract inventory are updated in the same checkpoint. Exactly zero changed files intersect the 21 production source members in `tinyrustlm/deploy/web-deployment-manifest.v2.json`; generated release bytes are also unchanged. No version, Updates entry, site deployment, release receipt, production readback claim, or campaign counter was created.
      campaign_truth: Live progress remains 0/20 qualifying models, 0/50 deletion-backed clean-browser rounds, 0/25 authorized live memory rounds, 0/40 outside-network lane proofs, and 0/20 catalog announcements.
      exact_resume_boundary: Add strict production HTTPS exact-byte readback as the next forward-only child of one admitted deployment attempt. Resolve and pin only public addresses, retain TLS hostname verification, forbid redirects and content encoding, request identity bytes, stream with exact byte bounds, require status/type/cache/security headers, hash every static response, and bind the complete observation inventory in one create-only `public-readback` receipt. Fixtures, loopback, LAN, caller responses, partial inventories, stale authorization, byte drift, and local-only reachability must never produce a public pass. Then generate and bind the two publication descendants, create evidence-bound published claim.v2 children, verify current site-publisher signatures, cut maintained entry points, and delete the old release stack without aliases.
    phase_3_public_https_verifier_checkpoint:
      status: Code commit `900a3f304f41ff92de851072679936e6a7acb3be` is pushed and remote-exact. It adds the sole standalone production HTTPS exact-byte verifier and frozen profile. It is not yet invoked by the release surface, writes no receipt, was not run against production, establishes no outside-network independence, changes no campaign counter, and does not complete Phase 3.
      exact_boundary: `performVnextPublicHttpsReadback` accepts only one canonical DNS HTTPS root, the sorted exact r5/v3 static inventory, and canonical frozen security-header bytes. It captures Node DNS/HTTPS at module load and accepts no caller resolver, transport, response, body, retry policy, success bit, or alternate profile. It rejects any malformed or nonpublic DNS member, pins one public address after bootstrap transport success, verifies each connected remote address, uses normal TLS hostname authorization and TLS 1.2/1.3, forbids redirects, content encoding, transfer encoding, ranges, cookies, and transformed bodies, and streams every asset once to exact length and SHA-256.
      web_and_header_contract: The verifier rejects project-hosted model routes, extra or invalid bootstraps, invalid service-worker policy, stable immutable paths, unsafe path encodings, unknown roles/media types/cache policies, and aggregate limit drift before DNS. Every response requires status 200, one canonical Content-Length, exact media type with only the bounded UTF-8 exception, exact cache control, the complete exact-once security-header profile, strong CSP/isolation/referrer/HSTS/nosniff/frame/permissions baselines, a complete response, and exact manifest bytes.
      result_and_privacy: The canonical result binds profile/source/security identities, origin, counts, sorted per-asset observations, and domain-separated complete-DNS-set, pinned-address, certificate-set, and observation-inventory identities. It returns no raw body, raw headers, address, certificate, credential, host path, or receipt authority. Pure exported fixture helpers cannot create the production result through caller-injected transport and never count as live readback.
      frozen_facts: Profile `contracts/vnext/public-https-readback-profile.v1.json` has SHA-256 `0cfeb00065d45fd860813f501d6c07417f8a6e6587dd524a81e5be426a84c419`; source SHA-256 is `b7d03950690761e63271dfca4734d16f75d5dd698f3c2326b29f2bf7f3dbf948`; test SHA-256 is `ee469ae358d0d93d3553dc69aeb99913f5ad6ad14388279d5c4326a11acf5fdc`; and the dedicated architecture account SHA-256 is `d1f9097a7ec159e16f9fa03c7b2f8f1cad2a7bf7c6cd5ad2d89bf62c4568581a`.
      verification: Focused verifier tests passed 9/9. The final fast affected contract/inventory/threat/redaction/verifier set passed 31/31 with zero failures, skips, cancellations, or todos. JavaScript syntax, frozen-profile self-validation, `git diff --check`, and a high-confidence sensitive-token scan passed. A redundant combined run containing the long synthetic release-surface suite was intentionally terminated after several quiet minutes to prioritize the user's requested source-control and site-release audit; no failure was emitted, but that terminated run is not counted as pass. The prior unchanged release surface remains proven at its pushed deployment-attempt checkpoint.
      documentation_and_deployment_truth: The dedicated architecture page, contract guidance, adjacent release-surface account, and threat addendum changed with the verifier. No production deployment-manifest member changed, and the verifier is not yet integrated into the immutable release receipt graph, so no version, Updates entry, deployment, production readback claim, or campaign counter was created. A seven-site audit reconfirmed that TinyRustLM.com 0.2.1 remains the only immutable successful-publication receipt; every other site retains its recorded ledger/test/tooling blocker.
      exact_resume_boundary: Extend only `tinyrustlm/tools/vnext-campaign-release-surface.js`. Reconstruct one admitted deployment attempt and current release authorization, invoke the production verifier inside that operation, scan canonical result and receipt bytes, and create-only persist redaction before one exact `public-readback` receipt with dependency-prefix crash recovery and byte-identical replay. Then add generated pointer/history, evidence-bound published claim.v2, and current site-publisher descendants; cut maintained entry points; and delete the superseded release stack without aliases or dual readers.
    phase_3_public_readback_admission_checkpoint:
      status: Code commit `316b74fac98863e2e37d9b78b3380902393c1dc3` is pushed and remote-exact. The sole release surface now invokes the frozen production HTTPS verifier only after exact stored deployment-attempt and current release-authorization reconstruction, independently validates the canonical result, scans finalized bytes, and create-only persists one forward-only `public-readback` receipt. The committed automated proof uses an explicitly fixture-labeled process substitution and is not a production run, public reachability receipt, outside-network proof, publication, or campaign evidence.
      exact_operation: `verifyPublicReadback` accepts one closed data-only input and rejects caller transport, response, resolver, verdict, pass, deployed, published, done, fixture, or counter truth. It reconstructs the prepared lineage and exact stored deployment receipt plus its deterministic redaction receipt, reruns current release-authorizer authority, invokes `performVnextPublicHttpsReadback` internally, and independently verifies every result descriptor, origin, count, asset observation, cache/security policy, response hash and length, DNS/certificate/address identity, verifier profile, and verifier source identity against the immutable r5/v3 manifest.
      persistence_and_recovery: The operation scans the canonical verifier result against the protected equality set, builds and scans the final receipt, and create-only persists the readback redaction before the readback receipt. Exact store prefixes admit the authorized deployment state, redaction-only interruption state, and complete readback state. Retry reruns the deterministic verifier, completes only byte-identical descendants, and returns an existing-byte-identical replay without overwriting history. Unrelated descendants, malformed results, unsafe bytes, stale authority, source drift, or dependency drift fail closed.
      frozen_facts: Release-surface profile is 11,271 bytes with SHA-256 `504492d9314b0751f09b2110b92cd7b2d5b284a11506b00b82d46ec9f0c4d7db`; release-surface source is 149,977 bytes with SHA-256 `d18bad2b0bdf478dbd2a0b7bde7f0f2af10bf041205d2bf366ff5f9c201b4a0d`; and its 90,784-byte test is SHA-256 `676e63b349ecd34dc706737b28d377676742d29b888fea7b5af1dd6afe402747`. The standalone verifier profile remains `0cfeb00065d45fd860813f501d6c07417f8a6e6587dd524a81e5be426a84c419`.
      verification: The final complete release-surface suite passed 22/22 with zero failures, skips, cancellations, or todos in 407.239 seconds. The adjacent receipt-store, redaction, and phase-currentness set passed 45/45; the contract, conformance, inventory, threat, verifier, and architecture set passed 41/41; and the final threat-review subset passed 3/3. JavaScript syntax, `git diff --check`, stale active-profile search, explicit debt-marker review, changed-file high-confidence secret scan, and exact production-manifest intersection passed. Zero of 21 production source members changed.
      documentation_and_deployment_truth: Contract guidance, release-surface architecture, verifier integration guidance, contract inventory, and threat review were updated in the same code commit. Because no production closure member changed and no authorized production operation was run, no version, Updates entry, deployment, release receipt, public readback claim, or campaign counter was created. All seven production URLs returned HTTP 200 after their intended redirects during the bounded availability audit; availability is not publication evidence.
